  "id": "old_roman_chant",
  "name": "Cantus romanus (Old Roman chant)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "The chant of the city churches of Rome, preserved in a small group of eleventh- to thirteenth-century manuscripts from Roman basilicas, among them the gradual of Santa Cecilia in Trastevere dated 1071; it shares most of its texts with the Gregorian repertory but sets them to its own melodies. Those melodies are more ornate and formulaic than their Gregorian counterparts, turning on recurring stepwise ornamental figures within a narrow range, and the tradition gave way to Gregorian chant in Rome during the thirteenth century. Modern reconstructions often sing it with a held drone beneath a florid solo line.",
  "recipe": "classic European early-1070s cantus romanus, ritual Devotional, christian gospel branch. many notes per syllable, solo over vocal choral drone, pure fifth stacked. drone plus voices sustained pedal under melody sacred liturgical global choir ensemble. parish church. blumlein stereo pair, ultra-clean solid-state pre. tape 15ips hall reverb.",
